# Git Workflow & Branching Strategy

## Core Principle: Each Phase = New Branch from Main

**Every phase starts fresh from the latest `main` branch.**

## Workflow

### 1. Complete Phase Work
```bash
# Working on phase-N-feature branch
git add .
git commit -m "Phase N: Feature description"
git push origin phase-N-feature
```

### 2. Create Pull Request
```bash
gh pr create --base main --head phase-N-feature --title "Phase N: Feature"
```

### 3. Merge to Main
- Review PR
- Merge to `main` via GitHub
- Delete phase branch after merge

### 4. Start Next Phase
```bash
# Pull latest main
git checkout main
git pull origin main

# Create new branch for next phase FROM MAIN
git checkout -b phase-N+1-feature

# Start work
```

## Branch Naming Convention

```
phase-1-foundation           # Phase 1
phase-2-basic-refactoring    # Phase 2
phase-3-release-v1.0         # Phase 3 (release)
phase-4-tasks-api            # Phase 4
# etc.
```

## Important Rules

### ✅ DO
- **Always branch from latest `main`**
- One branch per phase
- Create PR when phase is complete
- Merge to `main` before starting next phase
- Delete branch after merge
- Pull `main` before creating new phase branch

### ❌ DON'T
- Don't branch from another phase branch
- Don't work on multiple phases in parallel (unless independent)
- Don't merge phase branches into each other
- Don't reuse phase branches
- Don't start new phase before merging previous one

## Example: Complete Workflow

```bash
# ============================================
# Phase 1: Foundation (DONE)
# ============================================
git checkout -b phase-1-foundation
# ... work on Phase 1 ...
git add . && git commit -m "Phase 1 complete"
git push origin phase-1-foundation
gh pr create --base main --head phase-1-foundation

# Merge PR #2 via GitHub UI
# Delete phase-1-foundation branch

# ============================================
# Phase 2: Basic Refactoring (NEXT)
# ============================================
git checkout main
git pull origin main  # Get merged Phase 1 code

git checkout -b phase-2-basic-refactoring
# ... work on Phase 2 ...
git add . && git commit -m "Phase 2 complete"
git push origin phase-2-basic-refactoring
gh pr create --base main --head phase-2-basic-refactoring

# Merge PR via GitHub UI
# Delete phase-2-basic-refactoring branch

# ============================================
# Phase 3: Release v1.0
# ============================================
git checkout main
git pull origin main  # Get merged Phase 2 code

git checkout -b phase-3-release-v1.0
# ... prepare release ...
git add . && git commit -m "Release v1.0 preparation"
git push origin phase-3-release-v1.0
gh pr create --base main --head phase-3-release-v1.0

# After merge, tag the release
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git tag -a v1.0.0 -m "Release v1.0.0"
git push origin v1.0.0

# Continue with Phase 4...
```

## Why This Strategy?

### ✅ Benefits
1. **Clean History**: Each phase builds on stable `main`
2. **No Conflicts**: Avoid complex merge conflicts between phases
3. **Easy Review**: Each PR is self-contained
4. **Rollback Safe**: Can revert a phase without affecting others
5. **Parallel Work**: Different people can work on different phases

### ❌ Avoid
- Branching from feature branches creates dependency chains
- If Phase 2 branches from Phase 1 branch:
  - Phase 2 PR includes Phase 1 changes
  - Can't merge Phase 2 until Phase 1 is merged
  - Messy history and conflicts

## Release Phases

Release phases are special:

```bash
# Phase 3: Release v1.0
git checkout -b phase-3-release-v1.0

# Update version, docs, changelog
# Create distribution package
# Test thoroughly

git commit -m "Release v1.0 preparation"
git push

# After PR merge to main
git checkout main
git pull
git tag -a v1.0.0 -m "Release v1.0.0"
git push origin v1.0.0

# Build and distribute
gradle buildPlugin
```

## Hotfixes

For urgent fixes to a released version:

```bash
# Branch from main
git checkout main
git pull
git checkout -b hotfix/fix-critical-bug

# Fix and test
git commit -m "Fix critical bug"
git push origin hotfix/fix-critical-bug

# Create PR to main
gh pr create --base main --head hotfix/fix-critical-bug
```

